<h1>Cleated Belt Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A cleated belt conveyor uses raised profiles molded, vulcanized, or welded onto a flat belt to prevent products from sliding backward on inclines and to compartmentalize bulk materials for controlled feeding. Cleat profiles include T-cleats, L-cleats, chevron/V-patterns, and bucket-style formations. <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/solutions/cleated-belt-conveyor-solutions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner</a> rates cleated belts for inclines up to 60 degrees, and sidewall belts with cross cleats reach full vertical. Cleated belts serve industries from <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food processing</a> to mining, carrying everything from small parts and packaged goods to grain, aggregate, and recycled materials.</p>
<h2 id="why-heading">Why Conveyors Need Cleats</h2>
<p>Dorner limits standard flat belts to angles under 20 degrees. Above that, products slide, tumble, or roll backward, and bulk materials cascade down the belt. Patterned and rough-top belts help: <a href="https://www.forbo.com/movement/en-gl/products/conveyor-belts/siegling-transilon/top-face-patterns-and-textures/ppi6n3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Forbo Siegling</a> states certain patterned belts reach 30 degrees without profiles. The real limit depends on friction between the product and belt surface, so moisture, dust, and product bottom all matter.</p>
<p>Cleats solve this by creating physical barriers that support products against gravitational pullback. Each cleat acts as a shelf, ledge, or pocket that holds material in place regardless of belt angle. This makes cleated belt conveyors the standard solution for elevation changes in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a>, bulk material handling systems, agricultural processing, and recycling operations.</p>
<p>Beyond incline handling, cleats serve a second function: metering. Evenly spaced cleats create uniform compartments that deliver consistent material volumes to downstream processes. This metering capability makes cleated belts useful as feeders for weighers, fillers, counters, and batching systems, even on horizontal runs.</p>
<h2 id="profiles-heading">Cleat Profile Guide</h2>
<h3>T-Cleats</h3>
<p>T-cleats are straight, perpendicular bars that run across the belt width. They form a "T" cross-section when viewed from the side. T-cleats are the most common profile for conveying packaged goods, boxes, bags, and bundles. As one published example, <a href="https://www.condrives.com/product/hytrol-model-c-incline-beltconveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol's Model C</a> cleated incline uses a rough-top belt with 1 in. high cleats on 60 in. centers and installs at any angle from horizontal to 45 degrees. Spacing is set so each product rests against one cleat.</p>
<h3>L-Cleats</h3>
<p>L-cleats have an angled leading face that creates a scoop or ledge. The angled profile retains loose materials better than straight T-cleats and helps products self-center against the cleat face. L-cleats suit bulk granular materials and round products (bottles, cans, cylindrical containers) that would roll over a flat T-cleat.</p>
<h3>Chevron / V-Cleats</h3>
<p>Chevron (herringbone) cleats form V-shaped patterns across the belt surface. The V-pattern channels bulk materials toward the belt center, preventing edge spillage on inclines. Chevron belts are common for materials like grain, sand, fertilizer, and wood chips on moderate inclines, steeper than a smooth belt allows but below what full cleats with sidewalls reach. The V-pattern also adds grip for wet material.</p>
<h3>Bucket / Scoop Cleats</h3>
<p>Bucket-style cleats form deep pockets or scoops that fully contain material at steep angles. Combined with corrugated sidewalls, they convey at any angle up to 90 degrees (full vertical), per <a href="https://allstateconveyors.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/Continental_FLEXOWELL%C2%AE_Steep-Angle-Vertical-Conveying-System.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Continental's Flexowell literature</a>. Each pocket acts as an open-top bucket that fills at the bottom, carries material up the incline, and dumps at the top as the belt wraps around the head pulley. This configuration is called a sidewall belt conveyor and serves applications from grain elevators to foundry chip handling.</p>
<h3>Custom Profiles</h3>
<p>Manufacturers produce custom cleat profiles for specialized applications. Examples include cupped cleats for carrying liquids on inclines, perforated cleats for drainage applications, and ribbed cleats for friction-sensitive products. Custom profiles require minimum order quantities and longer lead times than standard T, L, and chevron options.</p>
