<p>These are manufacturer figures for their own products. Actual angles depend on material properties, belt speed, and cleat height, so test with real product. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="food-heading">Food-Grade Cleated Belts</h2>
<p>Food processing requires cleated belts made from materials that comply with FDA food-contact rules in <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-177" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR Part 177</a>. Food-grade cleated belts use polyurethane (PU) or silicone belt materials instead of rubber. These materials resist oils, acids, and cleaning chemicals while remaining non-toxic and non-absorbent.</p>
<h3>Welded vs. Glued Cleats</h3>
<p>Food-grade cleats are thermally welded (heat-sealed) to the belt surface, not glued. Welded cleats create a monolithic bond with no crevice between the cleat base and belt. Glued cleats create a seam where bacteria, moisture, and food particles accumulate. Welded cleats are the normal choice for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary conveyors</a>. Dorner, for example, describes radio-frequency welding its urethane cleats to the base belt to get a seamless bond.</p>
<h3>Detection-Friendly Materials</h3>
<p>Blue-pigmented belts are standard in food processing because blue is rare in natural foods, making belt fragments visually detectable if a belt degrades. Metal-detectable belt compounds contain additives that trigger <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">metal detection and reject systems</a> downstream. X-ray-detectable compounds provide an alternative for facilities using X-ray inspection instead of metal detectors.</p>
<h3>Sanitary Frame Design</h3>
<p>The belt is only part of the sanitary equation. Food-grade cleated conveyors need <a href="/conveyor-types/stainless-steel-conveyors/">stainless steel frames</a> (304 or 316 grade), tool-less belt removal for cleaning, sealed bearings, and open-frame construction that eliminates ledges where debris collects. Quick-release belt tensioners let operators remove the belt for cleaning without tools.</p>
<h2 id="sidewall-heading">Sidewall Belt Conveyors</h2>
<p>Sidewall belt conveyors combine corrugated flexible sidewalls with cross-cleats to carry bulk materials at steep angles, up to vertical. The sidewalls prevent edge spillage. The cross-cleats create pockets that contain material against gravity. Together, they form a continuous elevator that replaces <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a> in many applications.</p>
<p>Sidewall belts use a base belt with corrugated rubber or polyurethane walls bonded along both edges. The walls flex as the belt wraps around pulleys, then spring back to full height on the carrying run. Cleat spacing and height determine pocket volume and capacity. Continental lists Flexowell sidewalls and cleats from 40 mm (1.58 in.) to 630 mm high, which shows how far sidewall belts scale beyond packaging-size cleats.</p>
<p>Compared to <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">vertical reciprocating conveyors</a>, sidewall belt conveyors provide continuous flow rather than batch lifting. Compared to traditional bucket elevators, sidewall belts can run horizontal and inclined sections on one belt and are often easier to clean. The tradeoff is higher belt cost and more demanding belt tracking.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Specifying a Cleated Belt</h2>
<h3>Cleat Height</h3>
<p>Cleat height must exceed the product height for individual items, or contain the required material depth for bulk handling. As a reference, <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/3200-series/3200-belted/z-frame" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner's 3200 Series</a> offers cleats from 0.24 in. (6 mm) to 5.9 in. (150 mm) high. Taller cleats increase belt stiffness, require larger pulley diameters to maintain belt life, and reduce the belt's ability to wrap tightly around end pulleys.</p>
<h3>Cleat Spacing</h3>
<p>For discrete products (boxes, bags, bottles), set cleat spacing to product length plus enough clearance that the product drops in cleanly at the infeed. For bulk materials, spacing determines pocket volume. Closer spacing increases volumetric capacity per belt foot. Wider spacing reduces belt cost and allows easier cleaning. Coarse materials generally need wider spacing than fine materials.</p>
<h3>Belt Speed</h3>
<p>Cleated belt speeds are generally lower than flat belt speeds because of the impact loading at the feed point and the potential for product bouncing or shifting at high speeds. Published examples show the spread: Hytrol's Model C runs at a constant 65 FPM, while Dorner rates its 3200 Series cleated belts at up to 275 ft/min versus 600 ft/min for the flat belt version. Higher speeds require smoother transitions and longer infeed zones.</p>
<h3>Transition Zones</h3>
<p>Every cleated incline conveyor needs horizontal transition zones at the bottom (infeed) and top (discharge). The infeed zone lets products settle against the cleat before the incline begins. The discharge zone levels out before products leave the belt. Without adequate transitions, products shift, tumble, or fail to discharge cleanly. Size each horizontal section to hold at least one full product plus settling distance, and confirm with the supplier.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Cleated Belt Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Packaging line elevation changes:</strong> Cleated belt conveyors move packages between floor levels, over equipment, and between mezzanines. A <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> with a filler on the ground floor and a case packer on a mezzanine uses a cleated incline to bridge the height difference without an elevator.</p>
<p><strong>Agricultural products:</strong> Cleated belts convey fruits, vegetables, grains, and nuts on wash lines, grading lines, and packing lines. Chevron cleats prevent round produce from rolling backward. Food-grade polyurethane belts withstand the water, acids, and sanitizers used in produce processing.</p>
<p><strong>Recycling and waste handling:</strong> Cleated belts elevate mixed recyclables, shredded materials, and sorted fractions from ground-level collection to elevated sorting platforms. High-durability rubber belts with heavy-duty cleats resist abrasion from glass, metal, and aggregate.</p>
<p><strong>Parts feeding:</strong> Small manufactured parts (fasteners, stampings, molded components) ride in cleat pockets to orientation stations, counters, and assembly machines. Cleated belt feeders replace <a href="/conveyor-types/vibratory-conveyors/">vibratory bowl feeders</a> for parts that are too large, too heavy, or too fragile for vibratory feeding.</p>
<p><strong>Bulk material elevating:</strong> Sidewall cleated belts replace bucket elevators for sand, gravel, cement, grain, and animal feed. They accommodate larger particle sizes than many bucket elevators, and one belt can combine horizontal and inclined sections.</p>
<p><strong>Chip and scrap handling:</strong> Metal chips, turnings, and stamping scrap ride cleated belts from machining centers to chip bins. For heavier metalworking scrap, <a href="/conveyor-types/hinged-steel-belt/">hinged steel belt conveyors</a> provide greater durability than rubber cleated belts.</p>
<h2 id="maint-heading">Cleated Belt Maintenance</h2>
<p>Cleated belts require more maintenance attention than flat belts because of the cleats themselves. Key maintenance considerations:</p>
<p><strong>Cleat wear and damage:</strong> Cleats wear from abrasion, impact, and chemical exposure. Inspect cleats on a regular schedule for tears, delamination from the belt surface, and height loss. A worn cleat holds less product at the design angle, so replace worn cleats or belts before rollback starts.</p>
<p><strong>Belt tracking:</strong> Cleated belts are stiffer than flat belts and resist tracking adjustment. Improper tracking causes edge wear, cleat damage, and belt drift. V-guided belts use a V-shaped profile on the belt underside that rides in a corresponding groove on the slider bed, providing positive tracking without adjustment.</p>
<p><strong>Cleaning:</strong> Material lodges between cleats and in cleat-to-belt joints. In food applications, cleaning between and behind cleats is critical for sanitation compliance. Tool-less belt removal systems allow operators to pull the belt off the conveyor for full cleaning and inspection. </p>
<p><strong>Belt replacement:</strong> Cleated belts are typically endless (spliced into a loop) or joined with mechanical fasteners. Vulcanized splices are strongest but require field vulcanizing equipment. Mechanical fasteners allow faster field replacement but create a bump at the splice that affects product stability. Food-grade applications normally use welded or hot-press splices instead of mechanical fasteners.</p>
