<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a cleated belt conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A cleated belt conveyor has raised profiles (cleats) attached to the belt surface at regular intervals. Cleats prevent products from sliding backward on inclines and compartmentalize bulk materials for metered feeding. Profiles include T-cleats, L-cleats, chevron patterns, and bucket-style formations. Cleat height depends on the product and incline: Dorner offers 6 mm to 150 mm cleats on its 3200 Series, and bulk sidewall belts use much taller cleats.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What angle can a cleated belt conveyor handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It depends on the cleat profile, the product, and the belt design. Dorner rates its cleated belts for inclines and declines up to 60 degrees, compared with under 20 degrees for a flat belt. Hytrol's Model C cleated incline installs at any angle from horizontal to 45 degrees. Sidewall belts with cross cleats, such as Continental's Flexowell, convey bulk material at any angle up to 90 degrees (vertical). Test with real product to confirm the angle.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Are cleated belt conveyors food-safe?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Yes, when built for it. Food-grade cleated belt conveyors use belt materials that comply with FDA food-contact rules (21 CFR Part 177), cleats welded to the belt rather than glued, and stainless steel frames. Welded cleats remove the seam where food particles and moisture collect. Blue belts make fragments easier to see on food products.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What is the difference between cleated and sidewall belts?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Cleated belts have raised profiles across the width to prevent backsliding. Sidewall belts add corrugated walls along both edges to contain loose materials at steep angles. A combined sidewall-and-cleat belt can lift bulk materials like grains, powders, and granules up to vertical (90 degrees). Sidewall belts cost more than simple cleated belts but contain spillage at steep angles.</p>

</div>
</div>
